A||||14||fiero56||Comments/warnings before I consider BMW||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||11-16-2001||09:24 AM||||For the past few months I've been thinking it would be nice to have something bigger than just Fieros, and something different to drive once in a while.<P>For the past week or two I've been checking out classifieds for used BMWs. Particularly the mid/late '90s 3 series(325 in particular). These things can be had pretty cheap, are relatively low mileage, look good, can seat more than 2, etc...<P>Other than the fact that I see at least 5 of these cars at every street light on my commute every day, I like them.<P>I know little about the mechanicals on these cars. Is there anything I should be aware of about them? Engine/tranny/drivetrain problems, etc? Typical miles you can get before major engine work etc is needed?||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||11-16-2001||10:29 AM||||I know lots of people that have all models. One girl I know has leased 3 new 325's in last 6-8 years. Shes never had any problems with any of them. Beware though, that if it does need fixed, its not ever cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||11-16-2001||10:48 AM||||We just ordered our new BMW 325XI. BMW's are very reliable. As for cost to maintain, the answer is yes they are expensive. You cannot go to your local 5-minute oil place to get oil changes since they do not have the correct oil filters. You need to get one from a dealer or foreign car parts store that carry OEM BMW parts. Our new one comes with a 4-year mainatance contract (free when you buy a new or used certified BMW) plus the bumper to bumper 4 year warranty.<P>I know many people with BMW's that have lasted many years/miles without any major problems. If you want a used one I would suggest buying from a BMW dealer and getting one that is certified. You can get a maintance contract and warranty with them. (I think with used it 2 or 3 years not 4 like new). As for service, they have an excellent set-up. First when you take your car in, you are assigned a mechanic. He is the ONLY one who works on your car. (Until he either quits or retires) He keeps a history of your car in detail. If you buy from a dealer that has the original mechanic for the car, he continues to work on it.<BR>The dealer we bought ours from even has a chef cooking breakfasts and lunches. So you can bring it in for an oil change and wash and enjoy a nice breakfast.<P>BTW ~ Did I mention that the cars look and handle great? <P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Screwie||11-16-2001||10:51 AM||||Well, to be honest, a BMW normally doesn't have much problems. It's so-called German "Grundlichkeit". This is not just a cliche it's true. An older BMW is normally of very high quality if it has been driven normally. Parts are definitely relatively expensive, but you pay for quality.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WampusCat||11-16-2001||11:54 AM||||I dunno man, my friend Rich in Cleveland had to get the engine rebuilt in his '84 318...at 240,000 miles.  Check your local area for a BMW Car Club of America chapter.  They can give you lot's of info and leads to good mechanics. <A HREF="http://www.bmwcca.org/aboutCCA/abtfset.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bmwcca.org/aboutCCA/abtfset.shtml</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||11-16-2001||12:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>You need to get one from a dealer or foreign car parts store that carry OEM BMW parts. Our new one comes with a 4-year mainatance contract (free when you buy a new or used certified BMW) plus the bumper to bumper 4 year warranty.<P>He keeps a history of your car in detail. If you buy from a dealer that has the original mechanic for the car, he continues to work on it.<P>The dealer we bought ours from even has a chef cooking breakfasts and lunches. So you can bring it in for an oil change and wash and enjoy a nice breakfast.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yikes!  I mean, I LOVE german cars, but just the thought makes me squirm.  I know someday that won't seem to bad, but right now if I were to see the kind of pricetag you're talking about... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>I'll do my own work, thank you very much <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I can keep an excellent detailed history, change the oil, and be able to take a date out to dinner with the leftover (about 10 times).  As far as the hard things like driveability issues...they're almost non-existent anyway!<P>I fully support buying a BMW.  I don't have any personal notes to pass on, but I have worked with cars many years and have never heard one bad comment made about them (well, except for their price).  If you can afford it, you have nothing to lose!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-16-2001||01:17 PM||||I've been thinking about a car for a while. I've been flip-flopping about what I want. I'm still just a college student right now(at least 2 more years to go), so I'm not willing(or able) to drop a huge amount down on a new car. After seeing what new car prices were for even simple stuff like a new Grand Am(my original want), I realized I can get a nice used 3-series for less than half. As long as it is reliable, I don't care about extra maintance costs(you get what you pay for) as long as they aren't too terrible. <P>I've never heard anything negative about them. I'm looking for something I won't have to get dirty working on(=don't have to work on), and that I won't care or worry about taking it to the dealer(most people who know me know I will only go to dealers for service). Plus, they look good!<P>What are the typical maintanance issues with these cars? Other than the norm? I am still kicking the idea around, but am planning to visit at least one dealership this weekend.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||11-16-2001||09:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B> Yikes!  I mean, I LOVE german cars, but just the thought makes me squirm.  I know someday that won't seem to bad, but right now if I were to see the kind of pricetag you're talking about...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The BMW cost really isn't bad. It's no more expensive than the Durango we bought 4-years ago. The BMW is less than the price of a new Bonneville and the BMW is All-Wheel Drive. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||11-16-2001||10:51 PM||||My friend has a late 90's 328 or 528 (i forgot) and it is nice drives nice, handles nice, tons of room||||reg||1
Z||000009||GTFiero1||11-16-2001||11:57 PM||||older BMWs can be quite expensive to repair, my friend had one...83 i belive with a manual trans and it neededa new exauhst pipe... $1,000 . for a single exhaust. he just sold it since the exhaust was more than what paid for the car..thing was butt ugly anyway and the interior had a rank smell. good thing he got rid of it in my opinion. American is always cheaper to relpace... 98+ Cadillac Seville STS's aren't that expensive either<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000010||firstfiero||11-17-2001||11:48 AM||||     yep...parts are expensive! But they run forever. I had 230 thousand on my 80 528i and could still lay a patch in 3 gears And my fiero wouldn't be able to get near it in the twisties. That car was 15 years ahead of it's time. I've been thinking about buying a late 80's 3 series or another porsche 944 after christmas. I need something with a back seat also. I take the sportrac when I have to haul the kidies but my wife HATES driving the fiero and I'm tired of hearing her b**** about it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||11-18-2001||07:25 AM||||A friend of mine is a BMW/mercedes mechanic in Marin county...  Get a 90s or later BMW (any really) and enjoy!  (he makes a good living off a very scant few clients... ...)<P>They're bloody great cars, all of them really.  Anything made post 1990 will be reliable and *fast* and comfy and tight and satisfying.  <P>Be prepared to treat it like an *exotic* car (like a Fiero, dangit people!!) don't balk at what seem outrageous prices 'when i kould geet it for 1/10th for mah kam0-aero' ...<P>Get parts ONLY from the dealer.  Use Mobil 1 oil exclusively (and religiously.) DO NOT skip scheguled services... go in early, not late, etc...  And it'll last you the (second, Fiero remenber...) best 200,000 miles in your life!<P>If something does break, it will cost.  Good news is; nothing will likely break!!!<P>Used BMWs are worth every penny fifty times over (and they aren't even lots of pennys to buy really.)   They're a good deal new.  I mean, sure a 540 6-speed or an M5 costs around fifty-grand, but WHAT A CAR!  And that's not as much as many idoits pay for crappy SUVs.<P>Heck, a new Honda costs more than a used *V12* Bravarian Creame Puff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Me? I'm saving up for an 850Ci or ideally CSi.  Oh yes, an 850  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>3 series to have for me would be the post-97 (I belive. that generation at least) 328 cabriolet.  Oh, so nice!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||11-18-2001||09:05 AM||||It must be the area around here having so many BMWs, but they seem really cheap to me. Autotrader.com lists over 50 within 25 miles for less than $11k. And I don't live in a large city. Acutally, a '96 M3 with 56K on the clock just sold out from under me for $12K. I still got to check it out Saturday as the paper work was in-progress on it. Whoever bought that car got a DEAL! Most other 325s here for late '90s hover around the $8K-$10K mark. <P>I'm checking them out. But I'm also almost $2K into an engine rebuild, that isn't completed, so I think I should take it one thing at a time.<P>I wouldn't be surprised if I get one pretty soon though.||||reg||1
Z||000013||malacite||11-18-2001||07:49 PM||||BMW=Break My Window<P>be warned, bmw's make great targates for car theives. they are expensive enough to be worth somthing, but cheap enough to be plentyful. a perfect combenation.<P>i knew a guy who "worked" at a chop-shop and he said a bmw was the second most popular vehicle they "worked on" first being hondas.<P>the car itself though. German=Good<BR>i've noticed that...<P>------------------<BR>Malacite<P>Grendel-88 Fiero RIP<BR>'94 Saturn SW2 RallySport<BR>'95 Saturn SL1 Touring||||reg||12
Z||000014||LawAir||11-19-2001||09:20 PM||||I just aquired my first "bimmer" in the last 2 weeks.<P>It just had the timing belt done along with a new "top", cost about a grand.<P>Outside of a few goofy electrical fixes it has been pretty solid. (knock wood)<P>One of my initial concerns was repair cost, but a buddy of mine who owns a 12 bay independant garage here in town said there are enough of the old 325's running around to keep prices low.  (plenty of used "parts" and decent demand to encourage after market parts and competition.)<P>here is my webpage on it:<BR> <A HREF="http://ucsub.colorado.edu/%7Epattons/Home25.html" TARGET=_blank>http://ucsub.colorado.edu/%7Epattons/Home25.html</A> <BR>(follow the home link to see some links to my broither's fiero page along with v-8 archie's page)<P>There is considerible support on the net from bmw owner/mechs around the globe... this site is my favorite:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.bmwe30.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bmwe30.net/</A> <P>(online do-it-yer-self pix, etc)<P>cheers!  scott<P>||||reg||1
